    "available_at": "2026-10-15T14:38:29.949650+00:00",
    "body": "The Port of Savannah has won federal approval for a $2.3 billion container terminal that will add room to handle 3.5 million more twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs) a year. Construction is scheduled to begin in January 2028.\nWhat’s Related\nGeorgia Ports Authority President Kevin Price announced the permit approval on October 14 during the annual State of the Port of Savannah event. The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers approved the Savannah Container Terminal permit after a three-year review.\nThe new terminal will cover 350 acres on Hutchison Island in Savannah and is expected to be completed in the mid-2030s. Georgia Ports says the facility will be able to handle the largest container ships .\n“We’re excited about the Savannah Container Terminal’s role in our future growth and investment plans to take us to a 12 million TEU gateway container port, serving customers in Georgia and our nation with the best infrastructure in a high-growth region,” Price said.\nRELATED: Top 30 U.S. Ports Ranked By Container Imports\nMore room for containers\nSavannah can currently handle 7.75 million TEUs annually, according to Georgia Ports. The authority says its planned expansion projects will bring that figure to 12 million TEUs.\nThe Hutchison Island project is one part of that work. Georgia Ports is also spending $1.6 billion to upgrade Ocean Terminal, increasing its annual container handling limit from 200,000 TEUs to 1.75 million TEUs.\nThat project is expected to be completed by the end of 2028, several years before the new Savannah Container Terminal opens. The two projects will give Savannah additional space to handle containers on separate construction schedules.\nA larger port investment plan\nGeorgia Ports Chairman Alec Poitevint said the authority plans to invest $5 billion in future port infrastructure, following $4.13 billion spent over the past decade on port and inland infrastructure. The new container terminal is part of that longer-term spending plan.\n“We play the long game here,” Poitevint said.\nPrice also discussed the Talmadge Bridge, plans to widen and deepen Savannah Harbor, and the Gainesville Inland Port. Those projects were among the port and inland transportation updates presented alongside the newly approved terminal.",
